        <div style="margin-top:70px;">
          <!-- Guide 1 -->
          <section aria-label="Guide 1"><article class="story" id="golem-grad-boat-guide"><div class="story-label"><div class="story-number">Guide 01 · Planning</div><h2>How to plan a boat trip to Golem Grad</h2></div><div class="story-content"><p class="story-lede">A practical, step-by-step guide to visiting the island — from booking with a licensed operator to packing the right gear and respecting the protected landscape.</p><h3>When to go</h3><p>The best months for a boat trip are May, June, September, and early October. Summer (July–August) is hot but the lake is calm; spring can be windy and unpredictable. Winter crossings are often impossible due to weather.</p><h3>Booking with a local operator</h3><p>Boat trips to Golem Grad are arranged locally from the Prespa shore. Always use an authorised operator — they know the lake conditions, the protected area rules, and the best landing spots. Ask about the departure point, duration, and any access restrictions.</p><h3>What to bring</h3><p>Bring water, sun protection, a hat, closed shoes with good grip, and a small day bag. Pack light and take everything home with you. Leave speakers and drones behind — they disturb the island's tranquillity and wildlife.</p><div class="story-note"><strong>Important</strong><br>Check current access and visitor requirements with an authorised local operator or Galičica National Park before you travel. Rules vary by season.</div><h3>On the island</h3><p>Stay on permitted routes, follow your guide's advice, and leave plants, stones, and historical remains where they are. Observe wildlife from a distance and never feed or touch it. The island is a protected area — treat it with care.</p></div></article></section>
          <!-- Guide 2 -->
          <section aria-label="Guide 2"><article class="story" id="prespa-shore-guide"><div class="story-label"><div class="story-number">Guide 02 · Slow travel</div><h2>A slow guide to Prespa's shoreline villages</h2></div><div class="story-content"><p class="story-lede">The lake is the main attraction, but the villages along its shore have their own quiet magic. Here's how to explore them without rushing.</p><h3>Morning: coffee by the water</h3><p>Start in a village like Oteševo or Stenje. Find a café with a view of the lake, order a macchiato, and watch the fishing boats head out. The mornings are calm and the light is soft — perfect for slow observation.</p><h3>Midday: roadside fruit stands</h3><p>Prespa is famous for its apples. In season, you'll find small stands along the road selling fresh fruit, honey, and homemade preserves. Stop, buy something, and chat with the sellers — they're often happy to share stories about the region.</p><h3>Afternoon: a swim and a wander</h3><p>There are quiet swimming spots all along the shore. Ask locals for their favourite, or simply follow a path that leads to the water. After a swim, wander through the orchards or along the reed beds — the landscape is gentle and rewarding.</p><h3>Evening: the violet hour</h3><p>As the sun sets, the mountains turn mauve and the lake reflects the changing sky. Find a bench or a quiet spot near the water and just watch. This is the best time to be still.</p><div class="story-note"><strong>Slow travel tip</strong><br>Don't try to see everything. Pick one or two villages, spend time there, and let the day unfold. Prespa rewards unhurried attention.</div></div></article></section>
          <!-- Guide 3 -->
          <section aria-label="Guide 3"><article class="story" id="golem-grad-wildlife-guide"><div class="story-label"><div class="story-number">Guide 03 · Wildlife</div><h2>Wildlife watching on Golem Grad: tortoises, snakes, and birds</h2></div><div class="story-content"><p class="story-lede">The island is a haven for reptiles and birds. This guide will help you observe them respectfully and understand why Golem Grad is so ecologically important.</p><h3>Hermann's tortoises</h3><p>The island has one of the densest populations of Hermann's tortoises in the world. You'll see them basking on rocks, walking slowly through the juniper, or hiding in the shade. Watch from a distance and never pick them up — they are wild animals and stress easily.</p><h3>Dice snakes and nose-horned vipers</h3><p>Golem Grad is known as "Snake Island" for good reason. The dice snake is common near the water, while the nose-horned viper prefers rocky areas. Both are protected. Wear closed shoes, watch your step, and never attempt to handle a snake.</p><h3>Birds</h3><p>The island is an important nesting site for cormorants, herons, and egrets. In spring and summer, the colony is noisy and active. Keep your distance from nests and use binoculars or a zoom lens rather than approaching too closely.</p><div class="story-note"><strong>Respect wildlife</strong><br>Do not feed, touch, or chase any animal. Stay on the paths, keep noise to a minimum, and take all litter home. The island is a protected nature reserve — your visit should have no negative impact.</div></div></article></section>
