        <section aria-label="Article 16"><article class="story" id="what-the-monks-ate"><div class="story-label"><div class="story-number">Article 16 · Food & sustainability</div><h2>What the Monks Ate: Food, Fasting, and Survival on Golem Grad</h2></div><div class="story-content"><p class="story-lede">On a small island in Lake Prespa, a monastic community once fed itself without supermarkets, refrigerators, or imported goods. Their diet was simple, seasonal, and sustainable—and it offers surprising lessons for how we eat today.</p><p>Imagine trying to feed a community on a rocky island in the middle of a lake. There are no shops, no delivery trucks, no fields of wheat or orchards of fruit trees. There is only what the island provides and what the lake offers. This was the challenge facing the monks of Golem Grad for centuries.</p><p>Their solution was not deprivation but adaptation. They learned to eat what the land and water gave them, when they gave it. They learned to preserve, to store, to fast, and to feast. They learned that food is not a commodity to be bought and sold but a gift to be received with gratitude.</p><p>This is the story of what the monks ate—and what their diet can teach us about eating well in a world of abundance.</p><h3>The Island's Larder</h3><p>Golem Grad is small. It measures barely 20 hectares—roughly 50 acres—and much of that is rocky limestone, unsuitable for cultivation. But the island is not barren. It has resources, if you know how to use them.</p><p><strong>The Lake.</strong> Lake Prespa is rich in fish. The monks would have fished these waters, using simple nets and lines, catching species like carp, bleak, and the endemic Prespa trout. Fish provided protein, omega-3 fatty acids, and a food source that was available year-round, though the catch varied with the seasons.</p><p><strong>The Juniper Trees.</strong> The island is covered in ancient juniper trees, whose berries have been used for food and medicine for millennia. Juniper berries are bitter but nutritious, rich in antioxidants and natural sugars. The monks would have gathered them in autumn, drying them for use throughout the year. They could be used to flavour fish, to make tea, or to add to bread.</p><p><strong>Wild Greens.</strong> The rocky ground and thin soil support a surprising variety of edible plants. Wild greens like dandelion, chicory, and wild rocket would have been gathered in spring and early summer, when they were tender and abundant. These provided vitamins and minerals that supplemented the monastic diet.</p><p><strong>The Gardens.</strong> The monks would have cultivated small gardens in the sheltered spots on the island, growing vegetables that could survive the harsh conditions. Likely crops included onions, garlic, leeks, cabbage, and legumes like lentils and chickpeas. These were staples of the Mediterranean diet and could be stored for months.</p><p><strong>The Cistern.</strong> Water was always a concern on the island, but the Roman cistern collected rainwater, providing a reliable source for drinking, cooking, and watering the gardens. The monks would have used this water carefully, never wasting a drop.</p><h3>The Daily Diet</h3><p>What did a typical day's meals look like for a monk on Golem Grad? We can reconstruct it from what we know of Byzantine monastic diets and the resources available on the island.</p><p><strong>Breakfast (if any).</strong> In many monastic traditions, breakfast was not a formal meal. Monks might eat a piece of bread and drink some water or herbal tea before beginning their morning prayers and work. The emphasis was on moderation, not indulgence.</p><p><strong>The Main Meal.</strong> The main meal was usually eaten around midday, after the morning's work. It would have consisted of bread, fish, vegetables from the garden, legumes, olive oil, and wine diluted with water. Bread was the staple, made from wheat or barley flour. Fish was the primary source of protein. Vegetables and legumes provided sustenance and variety.</p><p><strong>The Evening Meal.</strong> If a second meal was eaten, it was lighter—perhaps bread with some olives or a simple vegetable dish. The monks did not eat meat, following the monastic tradition of abstinence.</p><h3>The Fasting Calendar</h3><p>The monastic diet was shaped not only by what was available but by the liturgical calendar. Fasting was a central practice of monastic life, and it structured the year.</p><p><strong>Weekly Fasts.</strong> In the Byzantine tradition, monks fasted on Wednesdays and Fridays, abstaining from fish, wine, and oil. On these days, they ate only bread, vegetables, and water. This weekly rhythm of fasting and feasting kept the community disciplined and mindful.</p><p><strong>Lenten Fasts.</strong> The Great Lent, before Easter, was the most rigorous fasting period. For forty days, the monks abstained from all animal products—fish, wine, and oil. Their diet consisted of bread, vegetables, legumes, and water. This was a time of purification and spiritual discipline.</p><p><strong>Other Fasts.</strong> Throughout the year, there were other fasting periods—the Advent fast before Christmas, the fast of the Apostles, and the fast of the Dormition in August. In total, the monks would have fasted for more than half the year.</p><p><strong>Feast Days.</strong> When the fasting ended, the feasting began. On Christmas, Easter, and other major feast days, the monks would eat more abundantly—fish, oil, wine, and perhaps special dishes prepared for the occasion. These feasts were celebrations of abundance after periods of restriction.</p><h3>Preserving the Harvest</h3><p>Without refrigeration, the monks had to preserve food to survive the lean months. They used techniques that had been developed over centuries and are still used today.</p><p><strong>Drying.</strong> Fish could be dried in the sun, preserving it for months. Vegetables and herbs were also dried, hanging in bundles from the rafters of the monastic buildings.</p><p><strong>Salting.</strong> Salt was a valuable commodity, brought from the mainland. It was used to preserve fish and vegetables, drawing out moisture and preventing spoilage.</p><p><strong>Fermenting.</strong> The monks likely fermented vegetables like cabbage and olives, creating foods that were not only preserved but enhanced in flavour and nutrition.</p><p><strong>Smoking.</strong> Fish could be smoked over juniper wood, giving it a distinctive flavour and extending its shelf life.</p><p><strong>Storing.</strong> The monks would have stored their preserved foods in cool, dark places—perhaps in the cistern or in cellars dug into the earth. Here, food could last for months, providing sustenance through the winter.</p><h3>The Spiritual Dimension of Food</h3><p>For the monks of Golem Grad, food was not just fuel. It was a spiritual practice.</p><p><strong>Gratitude.</strong> Every meal began with prayer, giving thanks for the food and for the hands that had provided it. The monks understood that food was a gift, not a right.</p><p><strong>Moderation.</strong> The monastic tradition emphasized moderation in all things, including food. The monks ate to live, not lived to eat. They were not ascetics who starved themselves, but neither were they gluttons. They found a middle way.</p><p><strong>Community.</strong> Meals were eaten together, in silence or with readings from scripture. The shared meal was a time of community, a reminder that the monks were not individuals but a body.</p><p><strong>Mindfulness.</strong> The monks paid attention to what they ate—where it came from, how it was prepared, what it meant. They did not eat mindlessly, as we often do today. They ate with intention.</p><h3>What the Monks Knew That We Have Forgotten</h3><p>The monks of Golem Grad lived in a way that is almost unimaginable to us today. They had no supermarkets, no restaurants, no food delivery apps. They ate what they could grow, catch, or gather. They preserved what they could not eat immediately. They fasted and feasted according to a rhythm that was older than any calendar.</p><p>And yet, there is much about their approach to food that we might do well to remember.</p><p><strong>Seasonal Eating.</strong> The monks ate what was available when it was available. They did not expect strawberries in December or tomatoes in January. Their diet was shaped by the seasons, and their bodies were attuned to the rhythms of the year.</p><p><strong>Local Sourcing.</strong> Everything the monks ate came from the island or the lake. There were no food miles, no carbon footprint, no industrial agriculture. Their diet was deeply connected to the place they lived.</p><p><strong>Minimal Waste.</strong> The monks wasted nothing. Every part of every plant and animal was used. Bones were boiled for soup. Vegetable scraps went to the compost. Nothing was thrown away.</p><p><strong>Preservation Skills.</strong> The monks knew how to preserve food without electricity. They dried, salted, fermented, and smoked. These skills, once common, are now rare. But they are skills that might become essential again in a world of climate change and resource scarcity.</p><p><strong>Mindful Consumption.</strong> The monks ate with awareness, gratitude, and moderation. They did not snack mindlessly or eat for entertainment. Food was a necessity and a gift, not a distraction.</p><h3>A Modern Monk's Diet?</h3><p>We cannot all live on a remote island and fish for our dinner. But we can learn from the monks of Golem Grad.</p><p>We can eat more seasonally, buying what is fresh and local rather than what is imported and out of season. We can reduce our food waste, using leftovers and composting scraps. We can learn to preserve food—to make jam, to pickle vegetables, to dry herbs. We can eat with more gratitude and awareness, recognizing that food is a gift, not a right.</p><p>The monks of Golem Grad did not have much. But they had enough. They ate simply, seasonally, and sustainably. They fasted and feasted. They gave thanks.</p><p>Perhaps that is a diet worth considering.</p><p><em>The monks of Golem Grad are gone. But their approach to food—seasonal, local, sustainable, and mindful—remains a model that is more relevant than ever. What they knew, we have forgotten. What they practiced, we might do well to relearn.</em></p></div></article></section>

