        <section aria-label="Article 17"><article class="story" id="island-as-metaphor"><div class="story-label"><div class="story-number">Article 17 · Cultural & artistic</div><h2>The Island as Metaphor: What Golem Grad Represents in Balkan Literature and Art</h2></div><div class="story-content"><p class="story-lede">A small island in Lake Prespa has never been a major setting in Balkan literature. But its name, its history, and its atmosphere have made it a powerful symbol—a container for questions about solitude, memory, and meaning.</p><p>There is a kind of place that exists in two worlds at once: the physical world of rock and water, and the imaginative world of story and symbol. Golem Grad is such a place. It is a real island, with real ruins, real tortoises, real snakes. But it is also an idea—a metaphor that writers, artists, and thinkers have returned to again and again.</p><p>What does Golem Grad represent? The answer depends on who you ask. For some, it is a symbol of isolation. For others, it is a monument to forgotten history. For still others, it is a paradox—a "Big City" that was never a city, a place of importance that has been abandoned.</p><p>This article explores the island as metaphor, tracing its presence in Balkan literature and art, and asking what it means to represent a place that is both real and imagined.</p><h3>The Paradox of the Name</h3><p>Before we can understand Golem Grad as metaphor, we must begin with its name. "Golem Grad" means "Big City" in Macedonian. But the island is small—barely 20 hectares—and it was never a city in any conventional sense.</p><p>This paradox is the island's first and most powerful metaphor. It is a name that speaks of ambition and loss, of greatness that has vanished, of a city that exists only in memory and stone.</p><p>In Balkan literature, names often carry this kind of weight. They are not arbitrary labels but compressed stories, reminders of what was and what is no longer. The name "Golem Grad" is a literary device in itself—an irony, a lament, a question. What does it mean to call a small island a big city? What does it mean to name something for what it once was, rather than what it is?</p><p>For writers, this paradox is irresistible. It invites exploration, interpretation, storytelling. The island's name is a door that opens onto larger questions about identity, memory, and the passage of time.</p><h3>The Island in Balkan Poetry</h3><p>Balkan poetry has a long tradition of engaging with landscape as metaphor. Mountains, rivers, and lakes are never just physical features; they are symbols of home, exile, memory, and loss. Islands, in particular, carry a special charge. They are places apart—isolated, self-contained, surrounded by water that both connects and separates.</p><p>Golem Grad appears in Balkan poetry less as a specific setting than as an image, a reference, a name that evokes a certain mood. Poets who have written about Prespa Lake often mention the island in passing—a dark shape on the horizon, a silhouette against the sky. It is not the subject of the poem but the backdrop, the frame that gives the poem its emotional depth.</p><p>In the work of Macedonian poets like <strong>Blaže Koneski</strong> and <strong>Aco Šopov</strong>, landscape is never neutral. The lake, the mountains, the island—these are not decorative elements but active presences, shaping the emotional and spiritual lives of the people who live near them. Golem Grad, though rarely named directly, is part of this imaginative geography. It is the unknown, the unreachable, the place that exists at the edge of consciousness.</p><p>For poets in exile—and the Balkans has produced many—the island becomes a symbol of home from which they are separated. It is a place they can see in memory but cannot reach. The water that surrounds it is the distance that separates them from their past.</p><h3>The Island in Prose</h3><p>In prose, Golem Grad has appeared more directly. Travel writers have described their visits to the island, often focusing on its atmosphere—the silence, the ruins, the sense of stepping into another time. These accounts are not fiction, but they are shaped by literary conventions. The island becomes a character in the narrative, with its own personality and mood.</p><p><strong>Robert Dacešin</strong>, a travel writer who visited Golem Grad, described it as "one hundred snakes per hectare" and "a forgotten world." His account is factual, but it is also evocative, using the island's wildlife and ruins to create a sense of adventure and mystery.</p><p>More significantly, the island has appeared in academic and historical writing, particularly in the work of archaeologist <strong>Vera Bitrakova-Grozdanova</strong>. Her two-volume monograph, <em>Golem Grad: Prespa</em>, is a work of scholarship, but it is also a work of literature in its own way. It reconstructs the island's history from the Neolithic period to the Ottoman conquest, but it does so with a sense of elegy, of loss, of time passing. The island becomes a metaphor for the transience of human endeavor—a "Big City" that was reduced to ruins.</p><p>In fiction, Golem Grad has been less visible, but its influence can be felt in the work of writers who have used islands as settings for exploring themes of isolation, memory, and identity. The island is not named, but it is there—a presence that shapes the story without appearing directly.</p><h3>The Island in Visual Art</h3><p>Golem Grad has been photographed, painted, and filmed. Each medium captures something different about the island.</p><p><strong>Photography.</strong> The island's visual power is undeniable—limestone cliffs, juniper trees, ancient ruins, the changing light of Lake Prespa. Photographers like <strong>Petar Alev</strong> have documented the island in all its moods, creating images that are both documentary and artistic. Alev's photographs, published in Bitrakova-Grozdanova's monograph, capture the island's textures, its colours, its sense of age. They are not just records of a place; they are interpretations of it.</p><p><strong>Painting.</strong> The island has been painted by Macedonian and Balkan artists, often in the tradition of landscape painting that emphasizes mood and atmosphere over realism. In these paintings, Golem Grad is not a specific place but a symbol of isolation, of beauty, of the passage of time.</p><p><strong>Film.</strong> The island has appeared in documentaries, most notably <strong>Luc Ronat's</strong> <em>Golem Grad, the Island of Reptiles</em> (produced for CNRS Images) and the 2026 documentary series <em>Europe: One Continent, Five Worlds</em>. In these films, the island is not just a subject but a visual metaphor—a place where nature and history intersect, where the human and the wild coexist.</p><h3>The Island as Metaphor: Four Interpretations</h3><p>What does Golem Grad represent? Here are four interpretations that emerge from the island's presence in Balkan literature and art.</p><p><strong>1. Isolation.</strong> The island is surrounded by water. It is separated from the mainland, from the villages, from the roads. This physical isolation makes it a powerful symbol of solitude—of the human condition of being alone, cut off, unreachable. In literature, islands often represent this kind of isolation. They are places where characters are stranded, where they confront themselves, where they discover who they really are. Golem Grad, with its ruins and its wildlife, is a particularly potent version of this metaphor. It is not just an island; it is an abandoned island, a place that was once inhabited but is now empty. Its isolation is not temporary but permanent.</p><p><strong>2. Memory.</strong> The island is full of ruins—churches, houses, cisterns. These ruins are physical traces of the past, reminders of the people who lived here and the lives they led. But they are also metaphors for memory itself: the way the past persists in the present, the way it shapes us even when we are not aware of it. In Balkan literature, memory is a central theme. The region has a long history of conflict, displacement, and loss. Memory is not just a personal matter; it is a collective one, a way of preserving identity in the face of erasure. Golem Grad, with its ruins and its name, is a symbol of this kind of memory. It is a place that remembers, even when no one is there to remember it.</p><p><strong>3. Transience.</strong> The island is called "Big City," but it is not a city. It was once a thriving settlement, but it is now abandoned. Its history is a history of rise and fall, of growth and decline, of life and death. This makes the island a symbol of transience—of the impermanence of all human endeavor. Empires rise and fall. Cities are built and abandoned. People live and die. The island is a reminder that nothing lasts forever, that even the greatest achievements are eventually reduced to ruins. In literature, this is a common theme. The ruins of ancient civilizations are often used to evoke a sense of melancholy, of the passage of time, of the vanity of human wishes. Golem Grad, with its name and its ruins, is a particularly powerful version of this metaphor.</p><p><strong>4. Paradox.</strong> The island's name is a paradox. It is called "Big City," but it is not a city. It was once important, but it is now abandoned. It is a place of ruins, but it is also a place of life—tortoises, snakes, birds, juniper trees. This paradox makes the island a symbol of contradiction, of the way things can be two things at once. It is both big and small, both important and forgotten, both dead and alive. In literature, paradox is often used to explore the complexities of human experience—the way we can feel two things at once, the way truth is often contradictory. Golem Grad is a paradox made place. It is a metaphor for the way we live with contradictions, the way we hold onto the past while living in the present, the way we find meaning in ruins.</p><h3>A Place That Exists in Two Worlds</h3><p>Golem Grad is not just a place. It is an idea. It exists in the physical world—a small island in Lake Prespa, with ruins and wildlife and a name that puzzles visitors. But it also exists in the imaginative world—a symbol, a metaphor, a container for questions about solitude, memory, transience, and paradox.</p><p>Balkan literature and art have engaged with the island in both these registers. They have described it as it is, and they have used it to explore what it means. In doing so, they have made Golem Grad something more than a place. They have made it a way of seeing—a lens through which we can look at the world and see something we might otherwise miss.</p><p>The island does not belong to any single interpretation. It is big enough, strange enough, old enough to hold many meanings. It is a "Big City" of the imagination, a place where stories are told and retold, where meanings are made and remade.</p><p>Perhaps that is the island's greatest metaphor: it is a reminder that places are not just physical. They are also stories, symbols, ideas. And the stories we tell about them are as real as the stones they are built from.</p><p><em>Golem Grad is a real place, but it is also an idea. It is a small island in a lake, but it is also a metaphor for solitude, memory, transience, and paradox. In the literature and art of the Balkans, the island has become something more than a place. It has become a way of seeing.</em></p></div></article></section>
