      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions.</h2>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>How do I get to Golem Grad?</h3>
          <p>Golem Grad is only accessible by boat from the Prespa shore. You should book with an authorised local operator. They will take you from a designated departure point, and the crossing usually takes 20–40 minutes depending on conditions. Always check access conditions before you travel.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Is it safe to swim in Lake Prespa?</h3>
          <p>Yes, swimming is generally safe in designated areas, but conditions vary by season and location. Avoid swimming in areas with strong currents, near reeds where snakes might be present, or where local advice advises against it. Never swim alone.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>What should I bring for a day trip to the island?</h3>
          <p>Bring water, sun protection, a hat, closed shoes with good grip, and a small day bag. Pack light and take everything home with you. Leave speakers and drones behind — they disturb the island's tranquillity and wildlife.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Are there snakes on Golem Grad?</h3>
          <p>Yes, the island is known as "Snake Island" for its population of dice snakes and nose-horned vipers. They are protected and generally avoid humans. Wear closed shoes, watch your step, and never attempt to handle or disturb them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Can I visit the island on my own?</h3>
          <p>No, you cannot visit independently. Golem Grad is a protected area within Galičica National Park, and access is only permitted with an authorised local boat operator. This is to protect the island's fragile ecosystem and archaeological remains.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>What is the best time of year to visit?</h3>
          <p>Late spring (May–June) and early autumn (September–October) offer the most comfortable conditions: mild temperatures, calm lake, and fewer visitors. Summer is hot but popular for swimming. Winter crossings are often impossible.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

